WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.580
Everyone's going to want to see Star Wars:
The Rise of Skywalker but some fans will need

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.250
to exercise a little extra caution.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:13.089
Disney is working with the Epilepsy Foundation
to get the word out that viewers with photosensitive

00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:17.680
epilepsy may be adversely affected by some
sequences in the movie.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.480
Those with photosensitive epilepsy a group
which comprises about 3 percent of epilepsy

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.390
patients overall can suffer seizures when
exposed to strobe-like flashing lights for

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.390
significant periods of time.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:34.000
As a result, The House of Mouse has reportedly
sent a letter to every venue exhibiting The

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.840
Rise of Skywalker, all over the world, warning
of the potential of some portions of the film

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.079
to cause serious problems for viewers with
the condition.

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:46.690
At the same time, the Epilepsy Foundation's
official Twitter account tweeted a message

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:51.120
praising Disney for its proactive stance,
and sharing a link to a page on its website

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.579
containing advice for photosensitive epilepsy
sufferers who, like the rest of us, are anxiously

00:00:56.579 --> 00:00:58.760
awaiting the flick's release.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:02.760
This is important info, which we'll share
for the benefit of anyone who suffers from

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.160
photosensitive epilepsy, or who knows somebody
who does.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.810
The Foundation's helpful message comes with
advice for viewers who may suffer from photosensitive

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:15.729
epilepsy, but still want to safely see the
movie, medical concerns or not.

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:17.250
The message reads, in part:

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:21.720
"If you or someone you know are sensitive
to lights, please consider the following as

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.850
you make plans to watch the movie:

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.710
-Ask a friend to watch the movie first.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.250
-Take your friend with you when you go see
the film to alert you to which scenes contain

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:35.060
the flashing lights so you block your eyes
during those scenes.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:41.689
-Teach your friend the three simple steps
of seizure first aid Stay, Safe, Side so that

00:01:41.689 --> 00:01:44.520
they can assist if you have a seizure."

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.009
About those three steps: in the event that
someone you're with has an epileptic seizure,

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:53.490
you'll want to Stay with the person and start
timing the seizure; make sure that they're

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:58.050
Safe, steering them clear of potentially dangerous
objects or environments; and turn them on

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.920
their Side to help make them as comfortable
as possible.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.140
You can visit the Epilepsy Foundation's First
Aid for Seizures page on its website for more

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.140
information.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.660
One of the last major pictures to pose significant
risks to those suffering from photosensitive

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:18.930
epilepsy was Incredibles 2, the highly-anticipated
sequel to Pixar's 2004 superhero spectacular.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.650
That film ended up carrying a similar warning
to The Rise of Skywalker's at some theaters.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:28.370
For its overseas and home releases, Disney
went so far as to re-edit the film so that

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:33.150
it would comply with the Harding Box test,
which is used to effectively measure the risk

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:37.020
posed to photosensitive epilepsy sufferers
due to flashing lights.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:43.090
2011's Twilight: Breaking Dawn Part 1 is another
major mainstream film which prompted a warning

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:48.319
from the Epilepsy Foundation after nine moviegoers
were reported to have been hospitalized during

00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:49.379
screenings.

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:53.970
The Tumblr page Movie Health Community, which
keeps track of films which might pose problems

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:58.890
for those with epilepsy or acute motion sickness,
has also compiled a list of recent films which

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.269
may prompt epileptic seizures.

00:03:01.269 --> 00:03:08.150
Among them are: Ocean's 8, Solo: A Star Wars
Story, A Wrinkle in Time, Deadpool 2, Ready

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:15.060
Player One, Avengers: Infinity War, Tomb Raider,
and the Netflix movie The Cloverfield Paradox.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.230
While it's only a small percentage of the
population that are adversely affected by

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:23.130
exposure to flashing lights, it's safe to
say that pretty much everybody is going to

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:28.129
be taking in The Rise of Skywalker at some
point, so it's only appropriate that the potential

00:03:28.129 --> 00:03:31.652
risk to that subset of viewers should be broadcast
loud and clear.

00:03:31.652 --> 00:03:35.349
"We're all in this, til the end."

00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:40.280
All too often, giant corporations are oblivious
to the issues that their products might cause

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.970
to underrepresented segments of their customer
bases, so when they get things right, we're

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.420
of the opinion that they should be commended.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:52.379
In this case, Disney absolutely got it right
so thank you, Mickey, for doing your part

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:56.569
to prevent any unnecessary suffering on the
part of viewers who can't wait to see how

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:59.980
the decades-spanning Skywalker Saga ends.

